-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">σ</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.538em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"rOSoIIOGva"},{"type":"text","value":", and since n, the sample size, appears in the denominator, increasing n will reduce the margin of error.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"Y21NrRQrSh"}],"key":"gLlcpaTD9C"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As we saw in our discussion about point estimates, probability theory tells us that the sampling distribution of ","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"Wm2BukN4ZU"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\bar{X}","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8201em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8201em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span></span><span style=\"top:-3.2523em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.1667em;\"><span class=\"mord\">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"a0hlHJc3zL"},{"type":"text","value":" is less spread out for larger samples:","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"jS1gbibIpg"}],"key":"k0F4pl7yEL"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m14-sample-size-prec-c19e9f2b3a15243fdeec657520ece330.svg","alt":"Two sampling distribution curves for the sample mean, both centered at the population mean. The curve from the larger sample size is tall and narrow, so its sample means are much more likely to be close to the population mean than those from the smaller sample.","key":"TRpFDN1Oxf","urlSource":"images/gen/m14-sample-size-precision.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"cRF6P1l442"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This explains why with a larger sample size the margin of error (which represents how far apart we believe ","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"XtrO1FzAZQ"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\bar{x}","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{x}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.2222em;\"><span class=\"mord\">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"sWU3bNhygp"},{"type":"text","value":" might be from μ for a given level of confidence) is smaller.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"DWfJz9CgrL"}],"key":"Fov7EK0MLF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"On an intuitive level, if our estimate ","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"FlbbrqOxZQ"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\bar{x}","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{x}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.2222em;\"><span class=\"mord\">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"QZHD2VybCf"},{"type":"text","value":" is based on a larger sample (i.e., a larger fraction of the population), we have more faith in it, or it is more reliable, and therefore we need to account for less error around it.","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"WDNA1WWgvU"}],"key":"NEtOztM0Z3"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"mCldTCjJGY"}],"key":"j97fUoXhTP"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"While it is true that for a given level of confidence, increasing the sample size increases the precision of our interval estimation, in practice, increasing the sample size is not always possible. Consider a study in which there is a non-negligible cost involved for collecting data from each participant (an expensive medical procedure, for example). If the study has some budgetary constraints, which is usually the case, increasing the sample size from 100 to 400 is just not possible in terms of cost-effectiveness. Another instance in which increasing the sample size is impossible is when a larger sample is simply not available, even if we had the money to afford it. For example, consider a study on the effectiveness of a drug on curing a very rare disease among children. Since the disease is rare, there are a limited number of children who could be participants. This is the reality of statistics. Sometimes theory collides with reality, and you just do the best you can.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Se185PNIE9"}],"key":"WpDYrwrohS"}],"class":"important","key":"gGQTp94PfD"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":66,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Margin of Error and Sample Size","position":{"start":{"line":66,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"key":"byUnguGdLX"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-margin-of-error-and-sample-size","label":"Check Your Understanding: Margin of Error and Sample Size","html_id":"check-your-understanding-margin-of-error-and-sample-size","implicit":true,"key":"R6bnmKfyfw"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-1352b69057e2d535d842eb760b60a8f5.mjs","model":{"question":"A 95% confidence interval based on n = 100 has margin of error 3. If everything else stays the same, what margin of error would a sample of n = 900 give?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1","feedback":"Correct! √900/√100 = 3, so the margin of error shrinks by a factor of 3: from 3 to 1."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/3","feedback":"Dividing by 9 would be right if n appeared without the square root; it's 1/√n."},{"correct":false,"text":"9","feedback":"Increasing the sample size DECREASES the margin of error."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The margin of error scales as 1/√n; the sample is 9 times larger.","explanation":""},"key":"ihJsyX0vdq"}],"key":"UYNkVN0UXw"}],"key":"JJQ2GJja6F"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Structure of a Confidence Interval","url":"/pages/m14-confidence-intervals-for-the-population-mean-4","group":"Section 11: Estimation"},"next":{"title":"Choosing a Sample Size for a Desired Margin of Error","url":"/pages/m14-confidence-intervals-for-the-population-mean-6","group":"Section 11: Estimation"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}